      },
      "text": "4 Filtering, smoothing, and prediction",
      "html": null,
      "level": 1,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.1209522977576788,
        "y0": 0.35025003266459986,
        "x1": 0.8790561017074433,
        "y1": 0.527211393412441
      },
      "text": "This section is devoted to optimal linear filtering, prediction and smoothing of partially observed polynomial processes. We let either 𝐼∶=ℕ or 𝐼∶=ℝ+ and fix a probability space (Ω,F,(F𝑡)𝑡∈𝐼,ℙ) as well as an ℝ𝑑-valued adapted process 𝑋= (𝑋(𝑡))𝑡∈𝐼. If 𝐼=ℝ+, we assume (F𝑡)𝑡∈𝐼 to be right-continuous. Suppose that the components 𝑋𝑚+1(𝑡),…,𝑋𝑑(𝑡) are observable whereas 𝑋1(𝑡),…,𝑋𝑚(𝑡) are not. We let the subscript o stand for the observable part of a vector 𝑥∈ℝ𝑑 and let 𝐻∶= (𝛿𝑚+𝑖,𝑗)𝑖=1,…,𝑑−𝑚;𝑗=1,…,𝑑, i.e. 𝑥o ∶=𝐻𝑥= (𝑥𝑚+1,…,𝑥𝑑). For Σ ∈ℝ𝑑×𝑑 we set Σ∶,o ∶= Σ𝐻⊤ = Σ1∶𝑑,𝑚+1∶𝑑, Σo,∶ ∶=𝐻Σ = Σ𝑚+1∶𝑑,1∶𝑑 as well as Σo ∶=𝐻Σ𝐻⊤ = Σ𝑚+1∶𝑑,𝑚+1∶𝑑. The subscript u standing for the unobservable part of a vector is treated in the same manner.",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.1209522977576788,
        "y0": 0.4703619237667629,
        "x1": 0.8790561017074433,
        "y1": 0.5958462506978346
      },
      "text": "We suppose that 𝔼(‖𝑋(𝑡)‖2)<∞ for 𝑡∈𝐼 and consider the following general filtering problem for fixed 𝑡∈𝐼. The goal is to minimise the mean square error 𝔼(‖𝑋(𝑡) −𝑌‖2) over all random variables 𝑌 that are measurable with respect to the observable information",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.44003621849360636,
        "y0": 0.5241539868628918,
        "x1": 0.4540952432149121,
        "y1": 0.6153202912494506
      },
      "text": "{",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.6056081548726978,
        "y0": 0.5241539868628918,
        "x1": 0.6196671795940035,
        "y1": 0.6153202912494506
      },
      "text": "}",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.3665157002802062,
        "y0": 0.5360391500077207,
        "x1": 0.44003621849360636,
        "y1": 0.6296547054840893
      },
      "text": "G𝑡 ∶=𝜎(",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.4540952432149121,
        "y0": 0.5360391500077207,
        "x1": 0.6043431954253153,
        "y1": 0.6272054543942795
      },
      "text": "𝑋o(𝑠) ∶𝑠∈𝐼, 𝑠≤𝑡",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.6196671795940035,
        "y0": 0.5360391500077207,
        "x1": 0.8790477022423212,
        "y1": 0.6296547054840893
      },
      "text": "). (4.1)",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.1209522977576788,
        "y0": 0.5830797372578366,
        "x1": 0.8790577816004678,
        "y1": 0.6772785043176661
      },
      "text": "We call the minimiser of (4.1) the optimal filter for . Regardless of any specific model the optimal filter is then given by the conditional mean 𝑋(𝑡,𝑡)̂ ∶=𝔼(𝑋(𝑡)|G𝑡).",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "heading",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.1209522977576788,
        "y0": 0.647236574849446,
        "x1": 0.597531229211324,
        "y1": 0.6701053581821853
      },
      "text": "4.1 Discrete-time linear filtering problems",
      "html": null,
      "level": 2,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.1209522977576788,
        "y0": 0.6535081780280083,
        "x1": 0.8670146285084568,
        "y1": 0.7446744824145672
      },
      "text": "Let 𝐼=ℕ. For Gaussian state space models, the optimal filter can be computed recursively:",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.1209522977576788,
        "y0": 0.6825012768889047,
        "x1": 0.8790409826702237,
        "y1": 0.7924087469859482
      },
      "text": "Proposition 4.1 (Kálmán filter). Suppose that 𝑋 is a linear Gaussian state space model as in Definition 3.1 and set 𝐶(𝑡) ∶=𝐵(𝑡)𝐵(𝑡)⊤. Let 𝑋(0,−1)̂ ∶=𝔼(𝑋(0)), Σ(0,−1)̂ ∶= Cov(𝑋(0)) and",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.2079640368501334,
        "y0": 0.7311537136680564,
        "x1": 0.52796517917739,
        "y1": 0.8253512929242538
      },
      "text": "𝑋(𝑡+̂ 1,𝑡 𝑎(𝑡+ 1) +𝐴(𝑡+ 1)𝑋(𝑡,𝑡),̂",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.21204113722038181,
        "y0": 0.7552138640439963,
        "x1": 0.7920342832568422,
        "y1": 0.8738623810711613
      },
      "text": "𝑋(𝑡,𝑡̂ 𝑋(𝑡,𝑡−̂ 1) + Σ̂∶,o(𝑡,𝑡− 1)Σ̂o(𝑡,𝑡− 1)+(𝑋o(𝑡) − 𝑋̂o(𝑡,𝑡− 1)), Σ(𝑡+̂ 1,𝑡 𝐴(𝑡+ 1)Σ(𝑡,𝑡)𝐴(𝑡+̂ 1)⊤ +𝐶(𝑡+ 1),",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.24332074533493708,
        "y0": 0.8037249521909038,
        "x1": 0.7092239566184426,
        "y1": 0.8979154046253073
      },
      "text": "Σ(𝑡,𝑡)̂ ∶= Σ(𝑡,𝑡−̂ 1) − Σ̂∶,o(𝑡,𝑡− 1)Σ̂o(𝑡,𝑡− 1)+Σ̂o,∶(𝑡,𝑡− 1)",
